Section name: Introduction
Excerpt:
As algorithmic decisions continue to influence critical aspects of modern human life, from resource allocation and recommendation to hiring and even predictive policing, the need to ensure fairness in data-driven systems has become increasingly urgent. Geometric approximation algorithms and notions such as 𝜀-nets facilitate Approximate Query Processing (AQP ) by providing compact data summaries, aka data representations, that preserve key proper- ties of large datasets [ 47,65]. These geometric tools are especially valuable due to their provable guarantees of approximation quality and computational efficiency. 𝜀-nets, for example, are well-known as small subsets of data that guarantee to contain at least one sample from each range larger than 𝜀, and hence can be used for fast query answering. To further clarify this, let us consider the following example for approximate database range-qu

